  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Chef is given a tree with NNN nodes and a positive integer KKK. Each node is assigned a value AiA_iAi​.\n\nChef has to divide the tree into a forest of exactly KKK trees, numbered from 111 to KKK, such that each node belongs to **exactly one** tree.\n\nLet BiB_iBi​ denote the bitwise XOR of the values of all nodes belonging to the ithi^{th}ith tree.\n\nFind the **maximum** possible value of B1B_1B1​ &amp;\\&amp;&amp; B2B_2B2​ &amp;…\\&amp; \\dots&amp;… &amp;\\&amp;&amp; BKB_KBK​, where &amp;\\&amp;&amp; denotes the bitwise AND operation.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line contains a single integer TTT, the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of multiple lines:\n\nThe first line contains two space-separated integers NNN and KKK — the number of nodes in the tree and a positive integer as mentioned in the statement, respectively.\nThe next line contains NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1,A_2,\\ldots,A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​, the values assigned to each node.\nThe next (N−1)(N-1)(N−1) lines describe the edges. The ithi^{th}ith of these (N−1)(N-1)(N−1) lines contains two space-separated integers uiu_iui​ and viv_ivi​, denoting an edge between nodes uiu_iui​ and viv_ivi​.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, print a single integer, the **maximum** possible value of B1B_1B1​ &amp;\\&amp;&amp; B2B_2B2​ &amp;…\\&amp; \\dots&amp;… &amp;\\&amp;&amp; BKB_KBK​",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤5⋅1041 \\leq T \\leq 5 \\cdot 10^41≤T≤5⋅104",
      "1≤K≤N≤2⋅1051 \\leq K \\leq N \\leq 2 \\cdot 10^51≤K≤N≤2⋅105",
      "0≤Ai≤1090 \\leq A_i \\leq 10^90≤Ai​≤109",
      "1≤ui,vi≤N1 \\le u_i, v_i \\le N1≤ui​,vi​≤N, (ui≠vi)(u_i\\neq v_i)(ui​=vi​)",
      "Sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 2⋅1052 \\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "2\n6 2\n1 4 2 2 1 5\n1 2\n1 3\n2 4\n2 5\n3 6\n8 3\n1 13 5 14 2 9 0 8\n1 2\n1 3\n1 4\n2 5\n3 6\n3 7\n6 8",
        "output": "6\n4",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: A possible way of dividing the nodes into 222 trees is [1,3,6][1,3,6][1,3,6] and [2,4,5][2,4,5][2,4,5].\n\nB1=A1⊕A3⊕A6=1⊕2⊕5=6B_1 = A_1\\oplus A_3\\oplus A_6 = 1\\oplus 2\\oplus 5 = 6B1​=A1​⊕A3​⊕A6​=1⊕2⊕5=6\nB2=A2⊕A4⊕A5=4⊕2⊕1=7B_2 = A_2\\oplus A_4\\oplus A_5 = 4\\oplus 2\\oplus 1 = 7B2​=A2​⊕A4​⊕A5​=4⊕2⊕1=7\n\nThus, B1B_1B1​ &\\&& B2=6B_2 = 6B2​=6 &\\&& 7=67 = 67=6.\nNote that this is the maximum possible value of B1B_1B1​ &\\&& B2B_2B2​ you can obtain.\nAnother way of dividing is [1,2,4,5][1,2,4,5][1,2,4,5] and [3,6][3,6][3,6], which leads to the same answer.\nTest case 222: A possible way of dividing the nodes into 333 trees is [1,3,6,7,8][1,3,6,7,8][1,3,6,7,8], [4][4][4], and [2,5][2,5][2,5]\n\nB1=A1⊕A3⊕A6⊕A7⊕A8=1⊕5⊕9⊕0⊕8=5B_1 = A_1\\oplus A_3\\oplus A_6\\oplus A_7\\oplus A_8 = 1\\oplus 5\\oplus 9 \\oplus 0\\oplus 8 = 5B1​=A1​⊕A3​⊕A6​⊕A7​⊕A8​=1⊕5⊕9⊕0⊕8=5\nB2=A4=14B_2 = A_4 = 14B2​=A4​=14\nB3=A2⊕A5=13⊕2=15B_3 = A_2\\oplus A_5 = 13\\oplus 2 = 15B3​=A2​⊕A5​=13⊕2=15.\n\nThus, B1B_1B1​ &\\&& B2B_2B2​ &\\&& B3=5B_3 = 5B3​=5 &\\&& 141414 &\\&& &\\&& 15=415 = 415=4.\nNote that this is the maximum possible value of B1B_1B1​ &\\&& B2B_2B2​ &\\&& B3B_3B3​ you can obtain."
      }
    ]
  },
